      "explanation": "Correct: (B) S phase, where S stands for synthesis. Interphase has three parts. In G1 the cell grows and carries out its normal functions. In S phase the cell copies all of its DNA, so each chromosome ends with two identical sister chromatids. In G2 the cell grows further, makes proteins needed for mitosis, and checks the copied DNA. The clue words DNA replication in the stem point to S phase.\n\n(A) is wrong because G1 is the growth phase that comes before replication begins, so the DNA content has not yet doubled during it.\n(C) is wrong because M phase separates chromosomes that were already copied; mitosis divides the DNA between nuclei and does not copy it.\n(D) is wrong because G2 follows the completion of replication, when the cell prepares for mitosis and checks for DNA errors, and no new copying occurs."
