      "explanation": "Correct: (D) G1, as part of interphase. In a typical dividing cell G1 is the longest phase: the cell grows, makes proteins and organelles, carries out its normal job, and waits for growth signals before committing to copy its DNA. Mitosis looks dramatic under a microscope but lasts only about an hour in a roughly 24 hour cycle, so most of the cycle is spent in interphase, and within interphase G1 is usually the longest part.\n\n(A) is wrong because cytokinesis is a brief final step that lasts a small fraction of the time of the whole cycle.\n(B) is wrong because S phase takes a substantial time, often around 8 hours, but it is usually shorter than G1, which can last 10 hours or longer.\n(C) is wrong because M phase is usually the shortest of the main phases, even though the visible chromosome movement makes it seem the longest."
